---
// Rendered iterations of THE READER'S RESULT COLUMNS (2026-08-25).
// House rule 10: two or three rendered iterations before anything
// reader-facing ships. Real helmets sample, not placeholder text, so the
// column heights are the real ones. Noindex, unlinked, no ads.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ReaderColumns from '../../components/labs/ReaderColumns';
---
<Base title="Reader columns (labs) - The Whetstone" noAds={true}>
  <Fragment slot="head"><meta name="robots" content="noindex" /></Fragment>

  <main class="max-w-[1600px] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-8 pb-24">
    <p class="font-mono text-[13px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-muted mb-2">The Whetstone · labs</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-[34px] text-ink-strong leading-tight mb-3">Reader columns</h1>

    <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-4 mb-10">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed">
        Four geometries for the same result. Switch between them and scroll each one: the thing to
        judge is what happens when you are 1,500 pixels down the findings, not what the top of the
        page looks like.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The problem, measured on the live page at 1440 pixels wide: the text column stops at 632
        pixels and the findings column runs to 2,763. About 2,100 pixels of blank page runs down the
        left while you read findings. The text panel does have its own inner scroll, but it does not
        stick, so once you scroll past it the text is gone entirely. That is what makes clicking a
        finding to see its quote feel broken.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-muted leading-relaxed">
        All four keep the two-way link, so click a highlight in the text or a card in the findings
        and the other side responds. Below 1280 pixels every variant collapses to the same stack,
        which is unchanged and not the thing being decided here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ReaderColumns client:load />

    <div class="max-w-3xl mt-14 space-y-6 border-t border-hairline pt-8">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[26px] text-ink-strong leading-snug mb-2">A · Text pinned</h2>
        <p class="text-[15px] text-ink leading-relaxed">
          The text holds still with its own scrollbar; findings scroll the page past it. This is the
          smallest change from what is live, and it is the pattern Create already uses for its two
          rails. What you are checking: whether a text panel that scrolls independently of the page
          feels like control or like a trap, especially when you scroll the page and the text does
          not move.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[26px] text-ink-strong leading-snug mb-2">B · Findings pinned</h2>
        <p class="text-[15px] text-ink leading-relaxed">
          The inverse. The article is the page and scrolls normally, the way a document should, and
          the findings ride alongside in their own scroller. Argument structure moves under the text,
          which is also where it belongs if the text is the spine. What you are checking: whether
          reading the piece straight through, with findings available rather than facing you, is the
          better default for someone auditing writing that is not theirs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[26px] text-ink-strong leading-snug mb-2">C · Three columns</h2>
        <p class="text-[15px] text-ink leading-relaxed">
          Exactly what Create runs: 28 / 44 / 28, structure left, text centre, findings right, both
          rails pinned. The strongest argument for it is that Read and Create would stop being two
          different shapes. The argument against is that 28 per cent of 1600 pixels is a narrow
          column for the argument skeleton, and the reading measure in the centre drops to about 44
          per cent. Worth checking on a 1280-wide laptop, not just here.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-[26px] text-ink-strong leading-snug mb-2">D · Three columns, rails track the page</h2>
        <p class="text-[15px] text-ink leading-relaxed">
          C with the scroll behaviour you asked for. In C each rail owns a scrollbar, so scrolling
          the page does nothing to it and the pointer has to be inside a rail to move it. Here the
          page scroll drives both rails, each through its own overflow at the same rate, so a short
          rail and a long one start together and finish together. The text holds still in the middle
          with its own scrollbar for when you want to read it rather than check it. There is no CSS
          for this: the standards issue asking sticky to scroll when taller than the viewport was
          closed without a solution, and the CSS-only answer everyone reaches for is what C already
          does. So it is a scroll listener, and the arithmetic behind it is unit-tested even though
          the scrolling itself could not be verified in the pane it was written in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Nothing on this page is live. Pick one and it gets built into the Reader.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
